<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sagala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sakala, Euthydemia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sagala was an ancient city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, generally identified with modern Sialkot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a> in the 2nd century BCE and is described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milindapanha">Milindapanha</a> as a prosperous city. Earlier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> had stormed the city of Sangala nearby, which some scholars equate with it.</p></div>
